@startuml
title `-3!` — a genuine ambiguity, PRESERVED rather than disambiguated early

skinparam backgroundColor #FEFEFE
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #374151
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.3
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #1F2937
  FontColor #111827
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #F9FAFB
  BorderColor #9CA3AF
}

rectangle "**SOURCE TEXT** `-3!`" as SRC #F3F4F6

rectangle "**LEX DAG** — the sign is at a FORK" as LEX #DCFCE7 {
  rectangle "edge A: one token\n`Int` literal `-3`\n(the `Int` pattern begins `-?`)" as LEX_A #A7F3D0
  rectangle "edge B: two tokens\nkeyword `-`, then `Int` literal `3`" as LEX_B #A7F3D0
}

rectangle "**PARSE FOREST** — <latex>Int</latex>-category readings" as SPPF #DBEAFE {
  rectangle "**Fact(NumLit(-3))**\n<latex>(-3)! = -6</latex>\nsign inside the numeral" as R1 #BFDBFE
  rectangle "**Neg(Fact(NumLit(3)))**\n<latex>-(3!) = -6</latex>\npostfix `!` (<latex>\ell = 37</latex>) binds\ntighter than prefix `-` (<latex>35</latex>)" as R2 #BFDBFE
}

rectangle "**LANGUAGE-LEVEL readings** (`CalculatorLanguage::parse`)" as LANG #EDE9FE {
  rectangle "**Fact(Neg(NumLit(3)))**\nreachable through a\ncross-category wrapper" as R3 #DDD6FE
}

rectangle "**ELECTION** — only for the SINGLE-result entry point\n`__mettail_wpda_select_min_weight_realizing`:\nglobal argmin over `LexicographicWeight`\n<latex>(\mathrm{primary},\ \mathrm{open\_len},\ \mathrm{lex\_alt},\ \mathrm{src\_idx},\ \mathrm{rule\_idx})</latex>" as ELECT #FEF3C7

rectangle "**BOTH readings survive** on the multi-result facades\n`Int::parse_via_wpda_all` and `…_all_with_weights`\n(exactly 2 `Int` alternatives — asserted, not observed)" as KEEP #DCFCE7

SRC -down-> LEX_A : maximal munch keeps\nBOTH edges in the DAG
SRC -down-> LEX_B

LEX_A -down-> R1
LEX_B -down-> R2
LEX_B -down-> R3 : with a `Proc` / `BigInt` /\n`BigRat` injection in scope

R1 -down-> ELECT
R2 -down-> ELECT
R1 -down-> KEEP
R2 -down-> KEEP

note right of KEEP
  **Why this is a feature.**
  The two `Int` readings DENOTE THE SAME NUMBER, so
  collapsing them at the lexer would remove an
  ambiguity rather than a meaning — and the project's
  standing rule is that an ambiguity belongs in the
  lattice.

  Removing it also had a measured cost: without the
  leading `-?` on the `Int` pattern, <latex>i32{::}\mathrm{MIN}</latex> had NO
  surface at all. Its <latex>\mathrm{display}</latex> is `-2147483648`, and the
  operator spelling does not exist for it, because
  `Neg`'s operand `2147483648` overflows <latex>i32</latex>.
end note

note bottom of ELECT
  The election is GLOBAL — it minimises over the whole
  derivation, so it can legitimately move as the
  expression grows. What the literal-domain invariant
  guarantees is that no motion of it can change the
  SPELLING of any token.
end note

@enduml
